-- title:
--   Double transitivity of k(t)-automorphisms on rational places
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field (with a decidable equality instance on $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,k$ assumed), and let $v,w$ be two places of $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,k$ over $k$, in the project's sense: each is a valuation subring of $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,k$ containing the image of $k$ under the structure map, different from the whole field, and a principal ideal ring. Assume $v \neq w$, and assume each of $v$ and $w$ is rational, i.e. $v$ is either `RationalFunctionField.placeInfty k` — the valuation subring of the degree (infinity) valuation on $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,k$ — or else $v =$ `RationalFunctionField.placeOfPoint k c` for some $c \in k$, the place attached to the height-one prime of $k[X]$ generated by the irreducible polynomial $X - C\,c$; and likewise for $w$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a $k$-algebra automorphism $\varphi$ of $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,k$ whose associated transport of places, `Place.congrRingEquiv` along the underlying ring isomorphism of $\varphi$ (which sends a place $u$ to the place whose valuation subring is the preimage of that of $u$ under $\varphi^{-1}$), carries `placeInfty k` to $v$ and carries `placeOfPoint k 0`, the place of the point $0$, to $w$.
--
--   This is the double transitivity of $\mathrm{PGL}_2(k)$ on $\mathbf{P}^1(k)$, expressed for the action of $k$-algebra automorphisms of the rational function field on its rational places; over an algebraically closed field the rationality hypotheses on $v$ and $w$ are automatic, and they are retained so that the statement holds over an arbitrary field. It is used in the construction of two-line degenerations in the Deligne–Rapoport model package for modular curves, where a pair of distinct rational places must be normalised to $\infty$ and $0$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_RationalFunctionField_exists_algEquiv_congrRingEquiv_placeInfty_eq_placeOfPoint_zero_eq.lean

